INSERT INTO questions (professions, course, topic, category, subtopic, difficulty, question_type, question_text, options, correct_answer, explanation, region, high_yield, year_level, source_reference) VALUES
('{"medicine"}','Medicine','Gastroenterology','Pathology','Oesophageal Disease','easy','mcq','Which type of epithelium replaces the normal squamous lining in Barrett''s oesophagus?','["A. Specialised intestinal columnar epithelium with goblet cells","B. Transitional epithelium","C. Pseudostratified columnar epithelium","D. Keratinised stratified squamous epithelium"]','A','Barrett''s oesophagus is defined by replacement of the normal stratified squamous epithelium of the distal oesophagus by specialised intestinal-type columnar metaplasia containing goblet cells, driven by chronic gastro-oesophageal reflux. This carries a risk of progression to adenocarcinoma.','universal',true,'{year1,year2,year3,year4,year5,year6,practitioner}','Robbins & Cotran Pathologic Basis of Disease, 9th Ed, Ch 17: Barrett Esophagus, p.757'),
('{"medicine"}','Medicine','Gastroenterology','Pathology','Oesophageal Disease','medium','mcq','A 55-year-old with a 20-year history of heartburn has endoscopic biopsy showing salmon-coloured mucosa with goblet cells 4 cm above the GEJ. What is the most feared long-term complication?','["A. Squamous cell carcinoma","B. Oesophageal adenocarcinoma","C. Gastrointestinal stromal tumour","D. MALT lymphoma"]','B','Barrett''s oesophagus predisposes to oesophageal adenocarcinoma via a metaplasia-dysplasia-carcinoma sequence at a risk of ~0.5% per year. Squamous cell carcinoma arises from native squamous epithelium, not Barrett''s metaplasia.','universal',true,'{year1,year2,year3,year4,year5,year6,practitioner}','Robbins & Cotran Pathologic Basis of Disease, 9th Ed, Ch 17: Barrett Esophagus/Adenocarcinoma, p.757-758'),
('{"medicine"}','Medicine','Gastroenterology','Pathology','Oesophageal Disease','medium','mcq','Achalasia results from degeneration of which neural plexus in the oesophageal wall?','["A. Meissners (submucosal) plexus","B. Perivascular sympathetic plexus","C. Auerbachs (myenteric) plexus","D. Enteric ganglia of the muscularis mucosae"]','C','Achalasia is caused by loss of ganglion cells in Auerbach''s (myenteric) plexus, leading to failure of lower oesophageal sphincter relaxation and absent peristalsis. Meissner''s plexus primarily regulates secretion and is not implicated.','universal',true,'{year1,year2,year3,year4,year5,year6,practitioner}','Robbins & Cotran Pathologic Basis of Disease, 9th Ed, Ch 17: Achalasia, p.753'),
('{"medicine"}','Medicine','Gastroenterology','Pathology','Oesophageal Disease','easy','mcq','On barium swallow, achalasia classically produces which radiological appearance?','["A. Rat-tail narrowing at the mid-oesophagus","B. Shouldering at the upper oesophagus","C. Corkscrew oesophagus","D. Bird-beak narrowing at the lower oesophagus with proximal dilatation"]','D','The classic barium swallow appearance in achalasia is smooth tapering ''bird-beak'' narrowing at the lower oesophageal sphincter with proximal dilatation. Corkscrew appearance is seen in diffuse oesophageal spasm.','universal',true,'{year1,year2,year3,year4,year5,year6,practitioner}','Robbins & Cotran Pathologic Basis of Disease, 9th Ed, Ch 17: Achalasia, p.753'),
('{"medicine"}','Medicine','Gastroenterology','Pathology','Oesophageal Disease','hard','mcq','Squamous cell carcinoma of the oesophagus most commonly arises in which anatomical location and is associated with which risk factors?','["A. Upper and middle two-thirds; smoking, alcohol, and hot beverages","B. Lower third; obesity and GORD","C. Lower third; achalasia and iron deficiency anaemia","D. Upper third only; Plummer-Vinson syndrome"]','A','Oesophageal SCC arises predominantly in the upper and middle two-thirds and is associated with smoking, alcohol, hot beverage consumption, and nitrosamines. Adenocarcinoma predominates in the lower third in the context of Barrett''s oesophagus and GORD.','universal',true,'{year1,year2,year3,year4,year5,year6,practitioner}','Robbins & Cotran Pathologic Basis of Disease, 9th Ed, Ch 17: Squamous Cell Carcinoma, p.759'),
('{"medicine"}','Medicine','Gastroenterology','Pathology','Oesophageal Disease','medium','mcq','A patient presents with haematemesis after repeated forceful vomiting. Endoscopy reveals a linear mucosal tear at the gastro-oesophageal junction without full-thickness perforation. What is the diagnosis?','["A. Boerhaave syndrome","B. Mallory-Weiss tear","C. Oesophageal variceal rupture","D. Cameron ulcer"]','B','Mallory-Weiss tear is a longitudinal mucosal laceration at or just below the GEJ caused by forceful retching, classically in alcoholics. Boerhaave syndrome is full-thickness perforation. Varices are submucosal dilated veins, not mucosal tears.','universal',true,'{year1,year2,year3,year4,year5,year6,practitioner}','Robbins & Cotran Pathologic Basis of Disease, 9th Ed, Ch 17: Lacerations, p.754'),
('{"medicine"}','Medicine','Gastroenterology','Pathology','Oesophageal Disease','hard','mcq','Which molecular alteration best describes early progression from Barrett''s oesophagus to adenocarcinoma?','["A. APC mutation followed by KRAS then TP53","B. MLH1 methylation leading to microsatellite instability","C. Early p16 (CDKN2A) loss followed by TP53 mutation and chromosomal instability","D. H. pylori-driven NF-κB activation and CDH1 loss"]','C','Progression from Barrett''s oesophagus involves early loss of p16 (CDKN2A tumour suppressor), followed by TP53 mutation and chromosomal instability/aneuploidy. This differs from the APC-KRAS-TP53 sequence of colorectal carcinoma.','universal',false,'{year1,year2,year3,year4,year5,year6,practitioner}','Robbins & Cotran Pathologic Basis of Disease, 9th Ed, Ch 17: Adenocarcinoma, p.758'),
('{"medicine"}','Medicine','Gastroenterology','Pathology','Oesophageal Disease','easy','mcq','A Zenker''s diverticulum forms at which anatomical site?','["A. Mid-oesophagus at the level of the carina","B. Gastro-oesophageal junction","C. Lower oesophageal sphincter","D. Pharyngo-oesophageal junction through Killians dehiscence"]','D','Zenker''s diverticulum is a pulsion (false) diverticulum through Killian''s dehiscence — a triangular weakness between the thyropharyngeus and cricopharyngeus muscles. It presents with dysphagia, regurgitation, and halitosis.','universal',false,'{year1,year2,year3,year4,year5,year6,practitioner}','Robbins & Cotran Pathologic Basis of Disease, 9th Ed, Ch 17: Esophageal Obstruction, p.753'),
('{"medicine"}','Medicine','Gastroenterology','Pathology','Oesophageal Disease','medium','mcq','Reflux oesophagitis most commonly produces which histological findings on biopsy?','["A. Basal cell hyperplasia, elongated papillae, and intraepithelial eosinophilia","B. Goblet cell intestinal metaplasia","C. Non-caseating granulomata in the lamina propria","D. Crypt abscesses and neutrophilic infiltration"]','A','Histological features of reflux oesophagitis include basal cell hyperplasia (>20% of epithelial thickness), elongation of lamina propria papillae (>66% of epithelial thickness), intraepithelial eosinophilia, and dilated intercellular spaces. Goblet cells indicate Barrett''s metaplasia.','universal',true,'{year1,year2,year3,year4,year5,year6,practitioner}','Robbins & Cotran Pathologic Basis of Disease, 9th Ed, Ch 17: Reflux Esophagitis, p.755'),
('{"medicine"}','Medicine','Gastroenterology','Pathology','Oesophageal Disease','hard','mcq','Which epidemiological feature best distinguishes oesophageal adenocarcinoma from squamous cell carcinoma?','["A. Adenocarcinoma incidence is declining in Western countries","B. Adenocarcinoma incidence is rising, associated with obesity and GORD, predominating in White males","C. Adenocarcinoma is more common in Black males","D. SCC is more common in White females over 60"]','B','The incidence of oesophageal adenocarcinoma has risen sharply in Western countries over four decades, linked to rising obesity and GORD. It predominates in White males. SCC is more common in Black males and populations with high tobacco and alcohol use.','universal',true,'{year1,year2,year3,year4,year5,year6,practitioner}','Robbins & Cotran Pathologic Basis of Disease, 9th Ed, Ch 17: Adenocarcinoma, p.758'),
('{"medicine"}','Medicine','Gastroenterology','Pathology','Gastric Disease','easy','mcq','Helicobacter pylori is best characterised as which organism and exploits which test for clinical identification?','["A. Gram-positive catalase-positive rod; CLO test","B. Gram-negative diplococcus; silver stain","C. Gram-negative urease-positive curved rod; urea breath test or CLO test","D. Gram-positive coccus; rapid antigen stool test"]','C','H. pylori is a Gram-negative, microaerophilic, urease-positive curved rod. Urease activity is exploited in the urea breath test and CLO rapid urease test. It colonises the gastric antrum primarily and is the leading cause of chronic gastritis and peptic ulcer disease.','universal',true,'{year1,year2,year3,year4,year5,year6,practitioner}','Robbins & Cotran Pathologic Basis of Disease, 9th Ed, Ch 17: Helicobacter pylori Gastritis, p.763'),
('{"medicine"}','Medicine','Gastroenterology','Pathology','Gastric Disease','medium','mcq','Which mechanism underlies NSAID-induced gastric ulceration?','["A. Increased acid secretion via histamine H2 receptors","B. Autoimmune destruction of parietal cells","C. Direct mucosal toxicity from ammonia produced by bacterial urease","D. COX-1 inhibition reducing prostaglandins and impairing mucosal defence"]','D','NSAIDs inhibit COX-1, reducing prostaglandin synthesis. Prostaglandins normally stimulate mucus and bicarbonate secretion and maintain mucosal blood flow. NSAID ulcers occur primarily in the stomach, are often asymptomatic, and may occur without H. pylori co-infection.','universal',true,'{year1,year2,year3,year4,year5,year6,practitioner}','Robbins & Cotran Pathologic Basis of Disease, 9th Ed, Ch 17: Peptic Ulcer Disease, p.766'),
('{"medicine"}','Medicine','Gastroenterology','Pathology','Gastric Disease','medium','mcq','A 68-year-old with epigastric pain and weight loss has biopsy showing discohesive cells with intracytoplasmic mucin vacuoles displacing the nucleus peripherally and diffuse gastric wall thickening. What is the diagnosis?','["A. Diffuse-type gastric adenocarcinoma with signet ring cells (linitis plastica)","B. Intestinal-type gastric adenocarcinoma","C. Gastrointestinal stromal tumour","D. MALT lymphoma"]','A','Diffuse-type gastric adenocarcinoma (Lauren classification) is characterised by signet ring cells — mucin-filled cells with peripherally displaced nuclei. Diffuse infiltration produces linitis plastica (leather-bottle stomach). It is associated with CDH1 (E-cadherin) loss and has a worse prognosis than intestinal type.','universal',true,'{year1,year2,year3,year4,year5,year6,practitioner}','Robbins & Cotran Pathologic Basis of Disease, 9th Ed, Ch 17: Gastric Adenocarcinoma, p.771'),
('{"medicine"}','Medicine','Gastroenterology','Pathology','Gastric Disease','hard','mcq','MALT lymphoma of the stomach is associated with H. pylori infection. What occurs after successful eradication of H. pylori?','["A. EBV reactivation causing lymphoma progression","B. Regression of low-grade MALT lymphoma in approximately 75% of cases","C. Worsening prognosis due to immune reconstitution syndrome","D. No change; chemotherapy is always required"]','B','Gastric MALT lymphoma is driven by antigen-dependent B-cell stimulation from chronic H. pylori infection. Eradication removes this antigenic drive and results in lymphoma regression in ~75% of low-grade cases. High-grade or t(11;18)-positive cases are less responsive to eradication alone.','universal',true,'{year1,year2,year3,year4,year5,year6,practitioner}','Robbins & Cotran Pathologic Basis of Disease, 9th Ed, Ch 17: Lymphoma, p.773'),
('{"medicine"}','Medicine','Gastroenterology','Pathology','Gastric Disease','easy','mcq','H. pylori gastritis most characteristically affects which part of the stomach?','["A. Fundus and body","B. Cardia only","C. Antrum primarily, with potential extension to pangastritis","D. Greater curvature uniformly"]','C','H. pylori preferentially colonises the gastric antrum where lower acid concentration favours its survival. Antral infection stimulates gastrin release and increases acid output, predisposing to duodenal ulcers. With time, infection can extend to pangastritis.','universal',true,'{year1,year2,year3,year4,year5,year6,practitioner}','Robbins & Cotran Pathologic Basis of Disease, 9th Ed, Ch 17: Helicobacter pylori Gastritis, p.763'),
('{"medicine"}','Medicine','Gastroenterology','Pathology','Gastric Disease','medium','mcq','Ménétrier''s disease is characterised by which combination of features?','["A. Small rugal folds, acid hypersecretion, and hypergastrinaemia","B. Diffuse lymphocytic infiltration and achlorhydria","C. Antral erosions, H. pylori positivity, and iron deficiency","D. Giant rugal folds, protein-losing gastropathy, and hypochlorhydria"]','D','Ménétrier''s disease features massive hypertrophy of gastric rugae (giant rugal folds), protein-losing gastropathy causing hypoalbuminaemia, and hypochlorhydria due to replacement of parietal cells by mucous cells. It is associated with TGF-α overexpression.','universal',false,'{year1,year2,year3,year4,year5,year6,practitioner}','Robbins & Cotran Pathologic Basis of Disease, 9th Ed, Ch 17: Ménétrier Disease, p.768'),
('{"medicine"}','Medicine','Gastroenterology','Pathology','Gastric Disease','hard','mcq','Which molecular alteration most commonly underlies hereditary diffuse gastric cancer syndrome?','["A. CDH1 (E-cadherin) germline mutation","B. APC germline mutation","C. MLH1 promoter methylation","D. KRAS codon 12 mutation"]','A','Hereditary diffuse gastric cancer is caused by germline CDH1 mutations. Loss of E-cadherin disrupts cell-cell adhesion, leading to diffuse infiltration of signet ring cells. Carriers have >80% lifetime risk of diffuse gastric cancer; female carriers also have elevated lobular breast cancer risk.','universal',true,'{year1,year2,year3,year4,year5,year6,practitioner}','Robbins & Cotran Pathologic Basis of Disease, 9th Ed, Ch 17: Gastric Adenocarcinoma, p.771'),
('{"medicine"}','Medicine','Gastroenterology','Pathology','Gastric Disease','medium','mcq','A patient has multiple refractory peptic ulcers and a fasting serum gastrin of 1200 pg/mL (normal <100 pg/mL). What is the most likely diagnosis?','["A. H. pylori-associated antral gastritis","B. Zollinger-Ellison syndrome (gastrinoma)","C. Autoimmune gastritis with pernicious anaemia","D. NSAID-induced gastropathy"]','B','Zollinger-Ellison syndrome is caused by a gastrin-secreting tumour (gastrinoma), most commonly in the pancreas or duodenum. Massive hypergastrinaemia drives extreme acid hypersecretion, producing multiple refractory ulcers often in unusual locations. It may be part of MEN1 syndrome.','universal',true,'{year1,year2,year3,year4,year5,year6,practitioner}','Robbins & Cotran Pathologic Basis of Disease, 9th Ed, Ch 17: Zollinger-Ellison Syndrome, p.769'),
('{"medicine"}','Medicine','Gastroenterology','Pathology','Gastric Disease','easy','mcq','Autoimmune gastritis most severely affects which part of the stomach and causes deficiency of which substance?','["A. Antrum; gastrin","B. Cardia; mucus production","C. Body and fundus; intrinsic factor leading to vitamin B12 deficiency","D. Pylorus; pepsinogen I"]','C','Autoimmune gastritis involves T-cell-mediated destruction of gastric parietal cells in the body and fundus, with antibodies against parietal cells and intrinsic factor. Loss of intrinsic factor impairs ileal B12 absorption, causing pernicious anaemia and achlorhydria.','universal',true,'{year1,year2,year3,year4,year5,year6,practitioner}','Robbins & Cotran Pathologic Basis of Disease, 9th Ed, Ch 17: Autoimmune Gastritis, p.764'),
('{"medicine"}','Medicine','Gastroenterology','Pathology','Gastric Disease','hard','mcq','Antral biopsy shows intestinal metaplasia with loss of normal glands in a 60-year-old male. This is a precursor for which carcinoma type via which pathway?','["A. Diffuse type via CDH1 loss","B. Intestinal type via APC → KRAS → TP53 sequence","C. Diffuse type via microsatellite instability","D. Intestinal type via the Correa cascade: H. pylori gastritis → atrophic gastritis → intestinal metaplasia → dysplasia → carcinoma"]','D','The intestinal type of gastric adenocarcinoma follows the Correa cascade: H. pylori gastritis → chronic atrophic gastritis → intestinal metaplasia → dysplasia → invasive carcinoma. This contrasts with diffuse type arising de novo via CDH1 loss.','universal',true,'{year1,year2,year3,year4,year5,year6,practitioner}','Robbins & Cotran Pathologic Basis of Disease, 9th Ed, Ch 17: Gastric Adenocarcinoma, p.771'),
('{"medicine"}','Medicine','Gastroenterology','Pathology','Coeliac Disease and Malabsorption','easy','mcq','Which antibody is the most sensitive and specific serological marker for coeliac disease?','["A. Anti-tissue transglutaminase IgA (anti-TTG IgA)","B. Anti-smooth muscle antibody (ASMA)","C. Antimitochondrial antibody (AMA)","D. Anti-gliadin IgG"]','A','Anti-TTG IgA is the most sensitive (~95%) and specific (~95%) serological test for coeliac disease. IgA deficiency (in ~2-3% of coeliac patients) can give false-negative results, so total serum IgA should also be measured.','universal',true,'{year1,year2,year3,year4,year5,year6,practitioner}','Robbins & Cotran Pathologic Basis of Disease, 9th Ed, Ch 17: Celiac Disease, p.782'),
('{"medicine"}','Medicine','Gastroenterology','Pathology','Coeliac Disease and Malabsorption','medium','mcq','Small bowel biopsy in coeliac disease shows which histological triad?','["A. Crypt abscesses, transmural inflammation, and granulomas","B. Villous atrophy, crypt hyperplasia, and increased intraepithelial lymphocytes","C. Goblet cell hyperplasia, Paneth cell loss, and submucosal fibrosis","D. Villous hyperplasia, reduced intraepithelial lymphocytes, and mucosal oedema"]','B','The classic histological triad of coeliac disease: (1) villous atrophy, (2) crypt hyperplasia, and (3) increased intraepithelial lymphocytes (>25 per 100 enterocytes). These findings are graded by the Marsh classification (grades 1-4).','universal',true,'{year1,year2,year3,year4,year5,year6,practitioner}','Robbins & Cotran Pathologic Basis of Disease, 9th Ed, Ch 17: Celiac Disease, p.782'),
('{"medicine"}','Medicine','Gastroenterology','Pathology','Coeliac Disease and Malabsorption','medium','mcq','Which HLA haplotypes are most strongly associated with coeliac disease?','["A. HLA-B27 and HLA-DR3","B. HLA-A3 and HLA-DR4","C. HLA-DQ2 (in ~90%) and HLA-DQ8 (in ~5-10%)","D. HLA-DQ7 and HLA-DR2"]','C','Coeliac disease is strongly associated with HLA-DQ2 (~90%) and HLA-DQ8 (~10%). These molecules present deamidated gliadin peptides to CD4+ T cells, driving the immune response. Approximately 30% of the general population carries these alleles without developing coeliac disease.','universal',true,'{year1,year2,year3,year4,year5,year6,practitioner}','Robbins & Cotran Pathologic Basis of Disease, 9th Ed, Ch 17: Celiac Disease, p.782'),
('{"medicine"}','Medicine','Gastroenterology','Pathology','Coeliac Disease and Malabsorption','hard','mcq','A 45-year-old woman with known coeliac disease develops abdominal pain, weight loss, and a jejunal mass. Biopsy shows large pleomorphic T-lymphocytes. What is the most likely complication?','["A. Colorectal adenocarcinoma","B. Mantle cell lymphoma","C. Primary intestinal follicular lymphoma","D. Enteropathy-associated T-cell lymphoma (EATL)"]','D','Enteropathy-associated T-cell lymphoma (EATL) is a rare but serious complication of coeliac disease arising from intraepithelial T-lymphocytes, typically presenting in the jejunum with abdominal pain or perforation. Patients with refractory coeliac disease are at highest risk.','universal',true,'{year1,year2,year3,year4,year5,year6,practitioner}','Robbins & Cotran Pathologic Basis of Disease, 9th Ed, Ch 17: Celiac Disease, p.782'),
('{"medicine"}','Medicine','Gastroenterology','Pathology','Coeliac Disease and Malabsorption','easy','mcq','Dermatitis herpetiformis associated with coeliac disease shows deposits of which immunoglobulin on skin direct immunofluorescence?','["A. IgA at the dermal papillae","B. IgM at the dermo-epidermal junction","C. IgG within the epidermis","D. IgE on dermal mast cells"]','A','Dermatitis herpetiformis is characterised by granular IgA deposits in the dermal papillae on direct immunofluorescence. It occurs in ~10% of coeliac patients and responds to a gluten-free diet. Lesions are intensely pruritic vesicles on extensor surfaces.','universal',true,'{year1,year2,year3,year4,year5,year6,practitioner}','Robbins & Cotran Pathologic Basis of Disease, 9th Ed, Ch 17: Celiac Disease, p.782'),
('{"medicine"}','Medicine','Gastroenterology','Pathology','Coeliac Disease and Malabsorption','medium','mcq','In which part of the small intestine is mucosa most severely affected in coeliac disease?','["A. Ileum","B. Proximal duodenum and jejunum","C. Mid-jejunum and proximal ileum","D. Duodenal bulb only"]','B','Villous atrophy is most severe in the proximal small intestine (duodenum and proximal jejunum) where dietary gluten exposure is greatest. The ileum is relatively spared. Standard diagnosis uses at least 4 biopsies from the second part of the duodenum (D2).','universal',true,'{year1,year2,year3,year4,year5,year6,practitioner}','Robbins & Cotran Pathologic Basis of Disease, 9th Ed, Ch 17: Celiac Disease, p.782'),
('{"medicine"}','Medicine','Gastroenterology','Pathology','Coeliac Disease and Malabsorption','hard','mcq','Tissue transglutaminase 2 (TG2) amplifies the immune response in coeliac disease by which mechanism?','["A. It cleaves HLA-DQ2 molecules exposing gliadin epitopes","B. It cross-links gliadin to tight junction proteins causing barrier disruption","C. It deamidates gliadin peptides increasing their affinity for HLA-DQ2/DQ8","D. It activates mast cells via IgE-mediated mechanisms"]','C','TG2 deamidates gliadin peptides by converting glutamine to glutamate residues. This deamidation substantially increases the affinity of gliadin peptides for HLA-DQ2/DQ8, amplifying CD4+ T-cell responses. TG2 is also the autoantigen targeted by anti-endomysial and anti-TTG antibodies.','universal',true,'{year1,year2,year3,year4,year5,year6,practitioner}','Robbins & Cotran Pathologic Basis of Disease, 9th Ed, Ch 17: Celiac Disease, p.782'),
('{"medicine"}','Medicine','Gastroenterology','Pathology','Coeliac Disease and Malabsorption','medium','mcq','A child has steatorrhoea, failure to thrive, and fat-soluble vitamin deficiencies. Biopsy shows villous atrophy with >25 intraepithelial lymphocytes per 100 enterocytes. Which additional finding would suggest refractory rather than untreated coeliac disease?','["A. Positive anti-TTG IgA serology","B. Crypt hyperplasia on biopsy","C. HLA-DQ2 positivity","D. Monoclonal T-cell population on flow cytometry despite strict gluten-free diet"]','D','Refractory coeliac disease type II is defined by persistent symptoms and villous atrophy despite strict gluten-free diet, associated with a monoclonal population of aberrant intraepithelial T-cells. It carries high risk of transformation to EATL.','universal',false,'{year1,year2,year3,year4,year5,year6,practitioner}','Robbins & Cotran Pathologic Basis of Disease, 9th Ed, Ch 17: Celiac Disease, p.782'),
('{"medicine"}','Medicine','Gastroenterology','Pathology','Coeliac Disease and Malabsorption','easy','mcq','The primary treatment for coeliac disease is:','["A. Strict lifelong gluten-free diet","B. Immunosuppression with corticosteroids","C. Methotrexate therapy","D. Prophylactic colectomy"]','A','The cornerstone of coeliac disease management is a strict lifelong gluten-free diet (avoiding wheat, barley, rye), leading to symptomatic improvement within weeks and histological recovery within months to years. Corticosteroids are reserved for refractory cases.','universal',true,'{year1,year2,year3,year4,year5,year6,practitioner}','Robbins & Cotran Pathologic Basis of Disease, 9th Ed, Ch 17: Celiac Disease, p.782'),
('{"medicine"}','Medicine','Gastroenterology','Pathology','Coeliac Disease and Malabsorption','hard','mcq','Which malabsorption syndrome is characterised by PAS-positive macrophages in the lamina propria of the small intestine?','["A. Coeliac disease","B. Whipples disease (Tropheryma whipplei infection)","C. Abetalipoproteinaemia","D. Autoimmune enteropathy"]','B','Whipple''s disease is caused by Tropheryma whipplei. The organism accumulates within macrophages in the intestinal lamina propria which stain intensely PAS-positive due to bacterial glycoproteins. Clinical features include malabsorption, arthralgia, lymphadenopathy, and neurological involvement.','universal',true,'{year1,year2,year3,year4,year5,year6,practitioner}','Robbins & Cotran Pathologic Basis of Disease, 9th Ed, Ch 17: Whipple Disease, p.791'),
('{"medicine"}','Medicine','Gastroenterology','Pathology','Inflammatory Bowel Disease - Crohn''s Disease','easy','mcq','Which macroscopic pattern of bowel involvement is characteristic of Crohn''s disease?','["A. Continuous involvement from the rectum","B. Diffuse colonic involvement without small bowel disease","C. Skip lesions — diseased segments separated by normal-appearing bowel","D. Disease limited to the sigmoid colon"]','C','Crohn''s disease is characterised by discontinuous skip lesions — areas of transmural inflammation interspersed with macroscopically normal segments. This contrasts with UC, which shows continuous mucosal involvement extending proximally from the rectum.','universal',true,'{year1,year2,year3,year4,year5,year6,practitioner}','Robbins & Cotran Pathologic Basis of Disease, 9th Ed, Ch 17: Crohn Disease, p.798'),
('{"medicine"}','Medicine','Gastroenterology','Pathology','Inflammatory Bowel Disease - Crohn''s Disease','medium','mcq','Which histological finding is most specific for Crohn''s disease rather than ulcerative colitis?','["A. Crypt abscesses","B. Mucosal ulceration","C. Goblet cell depletion","D. Non-caseating (epithelioid) granulomas"]','D','Non-caseating granulomas are found in ~30-60% of Crohn''s disease specimens and are virtually pathognomonic when present. They can occur in any layer of the bowel wall and in mesenteric lymph nodes. Crypt abscesses, ulceration, and goblet cell depletion are seen in both Crohn''s and UC.','universal',true,'{year1,year2,year3,year4,year5,year6,practitioner}','Robbins & Cotran Pathologic Basis of Disease, 9th Ed, Ch 17: Crohn Disease, p.798'),
('{"medicine"}','Medicine','Gastroenterology','Pathology','Inflammatory Bowel Disease - Crohn''s Disease','medium','mcq','Which depth of inflammation best describes Crohn''s disease?','["A. Transmural inflammation affecting all layers of the bowel wall","B. Mucosal and submucosal only","C. Submucosal only sparing the mucosa","D. Serosal predominance with minimal mucosal involvement"]','A','Crohn''s disease is characterised by transmural inflammation involving all layers — mucosa, submucosa, muscularis propria, and serosa. This predisposes to fistula formation (entero-enteric, entero-vesical, entero-cutaneous), abscesses, and fibrotic strictures.','universal',true,'{year1,year2,year3,year4,year5,year6,practitioner}','Robbins & Cotran Pathologic Basis of Disease, 9th Ed, Ch 17: Crohn Disease, p.798'),
('{"medicine"}','Medicine','Gastroenterology','Pathology','Inflammatory Bowel Disease - Crohn''s Disease','easy','mcq','The most common site of involvement in Crohn''s disease is:','["A. Sigmoid colon","B. Terminal ileum","C. Proximal jejunum","D. Rectum"]','B','The terminal ileum is the most commonly affected site in Crohn''s disease (~70% of cases). Ileocolonic disease (terminal ileum + right colon) is the most common overall pattern. Rectal involvement is uncommon in Crohn''s, contrasting with UC where rectal involvement is almost universal.','universal',true,'{year1,year2,year3,year4,year5,year6,practitioner}','Robbins & Cotran Pathologic Basis of Disease, 9th Ed, Ch 17: Crohn Disease, p.798'),
('{"medicine"}','Medicine','Gastroenterology','Pathology','Inflammatory Bowel Disease - Crohn''s Disease','hard','mcq','A patient with ileal Crohn''s disease presents with pneumaturia and faecaluria. What complication has occurred?','["A. Toxic megacolon","B. Perianal fistula","C. Entero-vesical fistula","D. Enterocutaneous fistula"]','C','Pneumaturia (gas in urine) and faecaluria are classic signs of an entero-vesical fistula — an abnormal communication between bowel and bladder caused by transmural inflammation penetrating adjacent structures. This is a recognised complication of ileal or colonic Crohn''s disease.','universal',true,'{year1,year2,year3,year4,year5,year6,practitioner}','Robbins & Cotran Pathologic Basis of Disease, 9th Ed, Ch 17: Crohn Disease, p.798'),
('{"medicine"}','Medicine','Gastroenterology','Pathology','Inflammatory Bowel Disease - Crohn''s Disease','medium','mcq','Cobblestoning of the intestinal mucosa in Crohn''s disease is caused by:','["A. Pseudopolyp formation from mucosal regeneration","B. Submucosal lipoma formation","C. Villous hyperplasia interspersed with crypt abscesses","D. Longitudinal ulcers intersected by transverse fissures overlying oedematous preserved mucosa"]','D','Cobblestoning in Crohn''s disease results from deep longitudinal rose-thorn ulcers intersected by transverse fissures, leaving islands of oedematous relatively preserved mucosa between them. This creates a cobblestone or paving-stone appearance on endoscopy and barium studies.','universal',true,'{year1,year2,year3,year4,year5,year6,practitioner}','Robbins & Cotran Pathologic Basis of Disease, 9th Ed, Ch 17: Crohn Disease, p.798'),
('{"medicine"}','Medicine','Gastroenterology','Pathology','Inflammatory Bowel Disease - Crohn''s Disease','hard','mcq','Which gene mutation is most strongly associated with Crohn''s disease susceptibility?','["A. NOD2/CARD15","B. MLH1","C. APC","D. CFTR"]','A','NOD2/CARD15 mutations (chromosome 16) are the most consistently replicated genetic risk factor for Crohn''s disease. NOD2 is an intracellular pattern recognition receptor for bacterial muramyl dipeptide. Loss of function impairs innate immune defence, contributing to dysregulated intestinal immunity.','universal',true,'{year1,year2,year3,year4,year5,year6,practitioner}','Robbins & Cotran Pathologic Basis of Disease, 9th Ed, Ch 17: Crohn Disease, p.798'),
('{"medicine"}','Medicine','Gastroenterology','Pathology','Inflammatory Bowel Disease - Crohn''s Disease','medium','mcq','A barium follow-through shows a ''string sign'' in the terminal ileum of a patient with Crohn''s disease. What does this represent?','["A. Mucosal oedema causing mild luminal narrowing","B. Severe luminal narrowing from oedema, spasm, or fibrotic stricture producing a thin barium stream","C. Cobblestone pattern from fissuring ulcers","D. Pseudopolyposis of the terminal ileum"]','B','The string sign represents severe luminal narrowing of the terminal ileum — either from acute oedema/spasm or chronic fibrotic strictures from transmural inflammation. It produces a thin linear stream of barium through the affected segment.','universal',true,'{year1,year2,year3,year4,year5,year6,practitioner}','Robbins & Cotran Pathologic Basis of Disease, 9th Ed, Ch 17: Crohn Disease, p.798'),
('{"medicine"}','Medicine','Gastroenterology','Pathology','Inflammatory Bowel Disease - Crohn''s Disease','easy','mcq','Which extra-intestinal manifestation is most commonly associated with both Crohn''s disease and ulcerative colitis?','["A. Primary sclerosing cholangitis","B. Ankylosing spondylitis","C. Peripheral arthropathy","D. Pyoderma gangrenosum"]','C','Peripheral arthropathy is the most common extra-intestinal manifestation shared by both Crohn''s disease and UC, occurring in 10-20% of patients and typically correlating with intestinal disease activity.','universal',true,'{year1,year2,year3,year4,year5,year6,practitioner}','Robbins & Cotran Pathologic Basis of Disease, 9th Ed, Ch 17: Inflammatory Bowel Disease, p.796'),
('{"medicine"}','Medicine','Gastroenterology','Pathology','Inflammatory Bowel Disease - Crohn''s Disease','hard','mcq','Fat wrapping (creeping fat) in Crohn''s disease refers to which macroscopic finding?','["A. Fatty infiltration of the submucosal layer","B. Steatosis of mesenteric lymph nodes","C. Lipoma formation within the bowel wall","D. Mesenteric adipose tissue encroaching around the serosal surface of the bowel"]','D','Creeping fat is a characteristic macroscopic feature of Crohn''s disease where mesenteric adipose tissue extends over and wraps around the serosal surface of the bowel. It reflects transmural serosal inflammation and is not seen in ulcerative colitis.','universal',false,'{year1,year2,year3,year4,year5,year6,practitioner}','Robbins & Cotran Pathologic Basis of Disease, 9th Ed, Ch 17: Crohn Disease, p.798'),
('{"medicine"}','Medicine','Gastroenterology','Pathology','Inflammatory Bowel Disease - Ulcerative Colitis','easy','mcq','Which pattern of bowel involvement is characteristic of ulcerative colitis?','["A. Continuous mucosal inflammation starting from the rectum and extending proximally","B. Skip lesions with transmural inflammation","C. Disease limited to the small bowel","D. Perianal disease with fistulae"]','A','UC invariably involves the rectum and extends proximally in a continuous, uninterrupted pattern. Inflammation is limited to the mucosa and submucosa. Skip lesions, transmural inflammation, small bowel involvement, and perianal disease are features of Crohn''s disease.','universal',true,'{year1,year2,year3,year4,year5,year6,practitioner}','Robbins & Cotran Pathologic Basis of Disease, 9th Ed, Ch 17: Ulcerative Colitis, p.800'),
('{"medicine"}','Medicine','Gastroenterology','Pathology','Inflammatory Bowel Disease - Ulcerative Colitis','medium','mcq','Pseudopolyps in ulcerative colitis are formed by:','["A. Adenomatous transformation of residual mucosa","B. Islands of regenerating mucosa surrounded by areas of ulceration","C. Hamartomatous overgrowth of the lamina propria","D. Submucosal lymphoid hyperplasia"]','B','Pseudopolyps in UC are non-neoplastic islands of oedematous, regenerating or residual mucosa projecting above surrounding denuded, ulcerated colonic mucosa. They carry no direct malignant potential but indicate active disease.','universal',true,'{year1,year2,year3,year4,year5,year6,practitioner}','Robbins & Cotran Pathologic Basis of Disease, 9th Ed, Ch 17: Ulcerative Colitis, p.800'),
('{"medicine"}','Medicine','Gastroenterology','Pathology','Inflammatory Bowel Disease - Ulcerative Colitis','medium','mcq','Toxic megacolon in ulcerative colitis is defined by transverse colon dilatation exceeding which diameter, combined with systemic toxicity?','["A. 4 cm","B. 8 cm","C. 6 cm","D. 10 cm"]','C','Toxic megacolon is defined as acute non-obstructive colonic dilatation with transverse colon diameter >6 cm on plain abdominal X-ray, combined with systemic toxicity (fever, tachycardia, leukocytosis, hypotension). It is a medical emergency with risk of perforation and peritonitis.','universal',true,'{year1,year2,year3,year4,year5,year6,practitioner}','Robbins & Cotran Pathologic Basis of Disease, 9th Ed, Ch 17: Ulcerative Colitis, p.800'),
('{"medicine"}','Medicine','Gastroenterology','Pathology','Inflammatory Bowel Disease - Ulcerative Colitis','hard','mcq','After how many years of extensive UC does colorectal carcinoma risk become clinically significant, and what precursor is targeted on surveillance biopsy?','["A. 5 years; crypt abscesses","B. 20 years; microsatellite instability","C. 15 years; pseudopolyps","D. 10 years; dysplasia (low-grade or high-grade)"]','D','CRC risk rises significantly after 10 years of extensive (pancolonic) UC. Surveillance colonoscopy targets dysplasia as the precursor lesion. High-grade dysplasia typically warrants colectomy. Cumulative CRC risk at 30 years of extensive UC is approximately 15-20%.','universal',true,'{year1,year2,year3,year4,year5,year6,practitioner}','Robbins & Cotran Pathologic Basis of Disease, 9th Ed, Ch 17: Colitis-Associated Neoplasia, p.801'),
('{"medicine"}','Medicine','Gastroenterology','Pathology','Inflammatory Bowel Disease - Ulcerative Colitis','easy','mcq','Which histological feature is characteristic of active ulcerative colitis?','["A. Crypt abscesses (neutrophil-filled crypts)","B. Transmural lymphoid aggregates","C. Non-caseating granulomas","D. Submucosal fibrosis"]','A','Crypt abscesses — accumulations of neutrophils within crypt lumina — are the hallmark of active UC, indicating acute mucosal injury. Chronic UC additionally shows crypt architectural distortion, goblet cell depletion, and Paneth cell metaplasia.','universal',true,'{year1,year2,year3,year4,year5,year6,practitioner}','Robbins & Cotran Pathologic Basis of Disease, 9th Ed, Ch 17: Ulcerative Colitis, p.800'),
('{"medicine"}','Medicine','Gastroenterology','Pathology','Inflammatory Bowel Disease - Ulcerative Colitis','medium','mcq','Primary sclerosing cholangitis (PSC) is most strongly associated with which form of inflammatory bowel disease?','["A. Crohns disease of the small bowel","B. Ulcerative colitis","C. Microscopic colitis","D. Indeterminate colitis"]','B','PSC occurs in approximately 5% of UC patients; conversely ~75% of patients with PSC have UC. PSC is characterised by periductal onion-skin fibrosis and stricturing of intra- and extrahepatic bile ducts, with risk of cholangiocarcinoma.','universal',true,'{year1,year2,year3,year4,year5,year6,practitioner}','Robbins & Cotran Pathologic Basis of Disease, 9th Ed, Ch 17: Ulcerative Colitis, p.800'),
('{"medicine"}','Medicine','Gastroenterology','Pathology','Inflammatory Bowel Disease - Ulcerative Colitis','medium','mcq','A ''lead-pipe'' colon on barium enema in long-standing UC is caused by:','["A. Perforation with extraluminal barium","B. Cobblestoning from fissuring ulcers","C. Loss of haustrations and foreshortening from chronic mucosal damage and muscularis hyperplasia","D. Pseudopolyps filling the colonic lumen"]','C','The lead-pipe appearance in chronic UC results from loss of normal haustral folds, colonic shortening, and rigidity due to chronic mucosal damage, submucosal fibrosis, and muscularis mucosae hypertrophy, producing a featureless tube-like colon on barium enema.','universal',true,'{year1,year2,year3,year4,year5,year6,practitioner}','Robbins & Cotran Pathologic Basis of Disease, 9th Ed, Ch 17: Ulcerative Colitis, p.800'),
('{"medicine"}','Medicine','Gastroenterology','Pathology','Inflammatory Bowel Disease - Ulcerative Colitis','hard','mcq','Which finding best differentiates ulcerative colitis from Crohn''s colitis on histology?','["A. Presence of crypt abscesses in UC but not Crohns","B. Pseudopolyps are seen only in UC","C. Goblet cell depletion is unique to UC","D. Transmural inflammation and granulomas favour Crohns; UC shows mucosal/submucosal disease with diffuse crypt distortion"]','D','Key histological differentiators: Crohn''s shows transmural inflammation, non-caseating granulomas, and skip lesions. UC shows mucosal/submucosal inflammation only, diffuse crypt architectural distortion throughout the colon, and no granulomas.','universal',true,'{year1,year2,year3,year4,year5,year6,practitioner}','Robbins & Cotran Pathologic Basis of Disease, 9th Ed, Ch 17: Ulcerative Colitis/Crohn Disease, p.798-800'),
('{"medicine"}','Medicine','Gastroenterology','Pathology','Inflammatory Bowel Disease - Ulcerative Colitis','easy','mcq','UC most consistently involves which part of the colon?','["A. Rectum (virtually always)","B. Transverse colon","C. Caecum","D. Descending colon only"]','A','The rectum is virtually always involved in UC. Disease extends proximally from the rectum in a continuous pattern — proctitis, left-sided colitis, or pancolitis. Rectal sparing strongly suggests Crohn''s disease.','universal',true,'{year1,year2,year3,year4,year5,year6,practitioner}','Robbins & Cotran Pathologic Basis of Disease, 9th Ed, Ch 17: Ulcerative Colitis, p.800'),
('{"medicine"}','Medicine','Gastroenterology','Pathology','Inflammatory Bowel Disease - Ulcerative Colitis','hard','mcq','Backwash ileitis in ulcerative colitis refers to which entity?','["A. Active ileal Crohns disease misdiagnosed as UC","B. Superficial ileal inflammation from reflux of colonic contents through an incompetent ileocaecal valve in pancolitis","C. Granulomatous ileitis found at right hemicolectomy","D. Small bowel bacterial overgrowth in UC"]','B','Backwash ileitis is superficial non-granulomatous ileal inflammation seen in up to 20% of patients with UC pancolitis, resulting from reflux of faecal contents through the ileocaecal valve. It should not be misinterpreted as skip-lesion Crohn''s disease.','universal',false,'{year1,year2,year3,year4,year5,year6,practitioner}','Robbins & Cotran Pathologic Basis of Disease, 9th Ed, Ch 17: Ulcerative Colitis, p.800'),
('{"medicine"}','Medicine','Gastroenterology','Pathology','Colorectal Carcinoma','easy','mcq','The adenoma-carcinoma sequence in colorectal cancer classically involves which sequence of molecular events?','["A. CDH1 → KRAS → TP53","B. MLH1 methylation → microsatellite instability → KRAS","C. APC loss → KRAS mutation → SMAD4 loss → TP53 mutation","D. BRCA1 → RAD51 → TP53"]','C','The Vogelstein adenoma-carcinoma sequence: APC loss (initiating adenoma formation) → KRAS activating mutation (adenoma growth) → SMAD4/DPC4 loss → TP53 mutation (malignant transformation). This chromosomal instability pathway accounts for ~85% of sporadic colorectal cancers.','universal',true,'{year1,year2,year3,year4,year5,year6,practitioner}','Robbins & Cotran Pathologic Basis of Disease, 9th Ed, Ch 17: Adenocarcinoma, p.810'),
('{"medicine"}','Medicine','Gastroenterology','Pathology','Colorectal Carcinoma','medium','mcq','A 25-year-old presents with hundreds of colorectal adenomatous polyps. Genetic testing reveals an APC gene mutation. What is the diagnosis and recommended management?','["A. HNPCC; annual colonoscopy","B. Peutz-Jeghers syndrome; surveillance only","C. Juvenile polyposis; endoscopic polypectomy","D. Familial adenomatous polyposis (FAP); prophylactic colectomy"]','D','FAP is caused by germline APC mutations (chromosome 5q21) and is characterised by hundreds to thousands of colonic adenomatous polyps. Without prophylactic colectomy, colorectal cancer is virtually inevitable by age 40-50. Gardner syndrome is a variant with extracolonic features.','universal',true,'{year1,year2,year3,year4,year5,year6,practitioner}','Robbins & Cotran Pathologic Basis of Disease, 9th Ed, Ch 17: Adenomatous Polyposis, p.809'),
('{"medicine"}','Medicine','Gastroenterology','Pathology','Colorectal Carcinoma','medium','mcq','HNPCC (Lynch syndrome) is caused by mutations in which genes, and what is its molecular hallmark?','["A. MLH1, MSH2, MSH6, or PMS2 (DNA mismatch repair genes); microsatellite instability","B. APC and KRAS; chromosomal instability","C. BRCA1 and BRCA2; homologous recombination deficiency","D. SMAD4 and TP53; loss of heterozygosity"]','A','Lynch syndrome (HNPCC) results from germline mutations in DNA mismatch repair genes — most commonly MLH1 and MSH2. Loss of mismatch repair function causes microsatellite instability (MSI-H), identifiable by tumour testing. Lynch syndrome accounts for ~3-5% of CRC and also increases risk of endometrial and ovarian cancer.','universal',true,'{year1,year2,year3,year4,year5,year6,practitioner}','Robbins & Cotran Pathologic Basis of Disease, 9th Ed, Ch 17: Hereditary Non-Polyposis Colorectal Cancer, p.810'),
('{"medicine"}','Medicine','Gastroenterology','Pathology','Colorectal Carcinoma','hard','mcq','In the Dukes staging system, which stage describes tumour extending through the muscularis propria into pericolic fat without lymph node involvement?','["A. Dukes A","B. Dukes B","C. Dukes C","D. Dukes D"]','B','Dukes staging: A = confined to bowel wall mucosa/submucosa; B = extends through muscularis propria into pericolic/perirectal fat, no nodal involvement; C = regional lymph node metastases; D = distant metastases. Dukes B carries approximately 65% 5-year survival.','universal',true,'{year1,year2,year3,year4,year5,year6,practitioner}','Robbins & Cotran Pathologic Basis of Disease, 9th Ed, Ch 17: Adenocarcinoma, p.810'),
('{"medicine"}','Medicine','Gastroenterology','Pathology','Colorectal Carcinoma','easy','mcq','Right-sided colorectal carcinoma typically presents with which clinical syndrome?','["A. Obstructive bowel symptoms and colicky pain","B. Rectal bleeding with mucus per rectum","C. Iron deficiency anaemia with occult bleeding","D. Tenesmus and change in bowel habit"]','C','Right-sided (proximal) colorectal cancers present insidiously with iron-deficiency anaemia from chronic occult bleeding, as the large-calibre right colon accommodates tumour growth without obstruction. Left-sided cancers present with obstructive symptoms, altered bowel habit, and overt rectal bleeding.','universal',true,'{year1,year2,year3,year4,year5,year6,practitioner}','Robbins & Cotran Pathologic Basis of Disease, 9th Ed, Ch 17: Adenocarcinoma, p.810'),
('{"medicine"}','Medicine','Gastroenterology','Pathology','Colorectal Carcinoma','medium','mcq','Which polyp type carries the highest risk of malignant transformation in the colorectum?','["A. Hyperplastic polyp","B. Juvenile polyp","C. Inflammatory polyp (pseudopolyp)","D. Villous adenoma"]','D','Villous adenomas carry the highest malignant potential (~40% risk if >4 cm). Malignant potential correlates with villous architecture, size >1 cm, and high-grade dysplasia. Tubular adenomas have the lowest risk (~5%); tubulovillous adenomas are intermediate.','universal',true,'{year1,year2,year3,year4,year5,year6,practitioner}','Robbins & Cotran Pathologic Basis of Disease, 9th Ed, Ch 17: Neoplastic Polyps, p.807'),
('{"medicine"}','Medicine','Gastroenterology','Pathology','Colorectal Carcinoma','hard','mcq','Which gene is mutated in the serrated adenoma pathway of colorectal carcinogenesis?','["A. BRAF (V600E mutation) with MLH1 methylation","B. APC","C. KRAS with SMAD4 loss","D. CDH1 with microsatellite stability"]','A','The serrated pathway involves BRAF V600E mutation and MLH1 promoter methylation (leading to MSI-H), occurring in sessile serrated adenomas/polyps. This pathway accounts for ~15-20% of sporadic CRC and predominantly produces right-sided, mucinous, MSI-H tumours.','universal',true,'{year1,year2,year3,year4,year5,year6,practitioner}','Robbins & Cotran Pathologic Basis of Disease, 9th Ed, Ch 17: Adenocarcinoma, p.810'),
('{"medicine"}','Medicine','Gastroenterology','Pathology','Colorectal Carcinoma','medium','mcq','The most common site of distant metastasis from colorectal carcinoma is:','["A. Lung","B. Liver (via portal vein)","C. Brain","D. Bone"]','B','Colorectal carcinoma most commonly metastasises to the liver via the portal venous system. Hepatic metastases are present at diagnosis in ~20-25% of patients. Resection of isolated hepatic metastases can be curative in selected patients.','universal',true,'{year1,year2,year3,year4,year5,year6,practitioner}','Robbins & Cotran Pathologic Basis of Disease, 9th Ed, Ch 17: Adenocarcinoma, p.810'),
('{"medicine"}','Medicine','Gastroenterology','Pathology','Colorectal Carcinoma','easy','mcq','Which tumour marker is most commonly monitored for colorectal carcinoma recurrence after surgery?','["A. CA 19-9","B. AFP (alpha-fetoprotein)","C. CEA (carcinoembryonic antigen)","D. CA 125"]','C','CEA (carcinoembryonic antigen) is the most commonly used serum marker for monitoring colorectal carcinoma after resection. Rising CEA levels post-surgery suggest recurrence. However, CEA lacks sufficient specificity for primary diagnosis or screening.','universal',true,'{year1,year2,year3,year4,year5,year6,practitioner}','Robbins & Cotran Pathologic Basis of Disease, 9th Ed, Ch 17: Adenocarcinoma, p.810'),
('{"medicine"}','Medicine','Gastroenterology','Pathology','Colorectal Carcinoma','hard','mcq','Left-sided colorectal carcinoma classically produces which radiological appearance on barium enema?','["A. Lead-pipe appearance","B. Cobblestone pattern","C. Bird-beak narrowing","D. Apple-core (napkin-ring) lesion"]','D','Left-sided colorectal cancers grow circumferentially around the bowel wall, producing an apple-core or napkin-ring appearance on barium enema — a short segment of annular stricture with overhanging mucosal edges (shouldering). This leads to progressive obstructive symptoms.','universal',true,'{year1,year2,year3,year4,year5,year6,practitioner}','Robbins & Cotran Pathologic Basis of Disease, 9th Ed, Ch 17: Adenocarcinoma, p.810'),
('{"medicine"}','Medicine','Gastroenterology','Pathology','Liver Disease - Hepatitis','easy','mcq','Which serological marker is the first to appear in acute hepatitis B infection and indicates active HBV?','["A. HBsAg (hepatitis B surface antigen)","B. Anti-HBs","C. IgG anti-HBc","D. HBeAg"]','A','HBsAg is the first serological marker to appear in acute HBV infection (4-12 weeks post-exposure) and indicates active infection. It persists in chronic carriers. Anti-HBs indicates immunity. IgM anti-HBc indicates recent acute infection.','universal',true,'{year1,year2,year3,year4,year5,year6,practitioner}','Robbins & Cotran Pathologic Basis of Disease, 9th Ed, Ch 18: Viral Hepatitis, p.836'),
('{"medicine"}','Medicine','Gastroenterology','Pathology','Liver Disease - Hepatitis','medium','mcq','Which serological marker of hepatitis B indicates high viral replication and high infectivity?','["A. Anti-HBs","B. HBeAg","C. Anti-HBe","D. IgG anti-HBc"]','B','HBeAg (hepatitis B e antigen) is a secreted viral protein indicating active replication and high infectivity. Its presence in a chronic carrier indicates ongoing hepatocyte damage and high transmissibility. Seroconversion from HBeAg to anti-HBe indicates reduced viral replication.','universal',true,'{year1,year2,year3,year4,year5,year6,practitioner}','Robbins & Cotran Pathologic Basis of Disease, 9th Ed, Ch 18: Viral Hepatitis, p.836'),
('{"medicine"}','Medicine','Gastroenterology','Pathology','Liver Disease - Hepatitis','medium','mcq','Hepatitis A virus infection is correctly characterised by which statement?','["A. It is transmitted by blood transfusion and causes chronic infection","B. It requires HBV co-infection to replicate","C. It is transmitted faeco-orally, causes acute self-limiting hepatitis, and does not cause chronic infection","D. It causes fulminant hepatitis in 20% of cases"]','C','HAV is transmitted faeco-orally (contaminated food/water), causes acute self-limiting hepatitis, and does not establish chronic infection. IgM anti-HAV indicates acute infection; IgG anti-HAV indicates past infection or immunity. Fulminant hepatic failure occurs in <1% of cases.','universal',true,'{year1,year2,year3,year4,year5,year6,practitioner}','Robbins & Cotran Pathologic Basis of Disease, 9th Ed, Ch 18: Viral Hepatitis, p.836'),
('{"medicine"}','Medicine','Gastroenterology','Pathology','Liver Disease - Hepatitis','hard','mcq','What proportion of HCV-infected individuals develop chronic infection, and which genotype is most prevalent globally?','["A. 10-20%; genotype 2","B. >95%; genotype 4","C. 50%; genotype 3","D. 75-85%; genotype 1"]','D','Approximately 75-85% of HCV-infected individuals develop chronic infection — the highest chronicity rate of any hepatitis virus. Genotype 1 is most common globally and historically most difficult to treat, though direct-acting antivirals achieve >95% cure rates across genotypes.','universal',true,'{year1,year2,year3,year4,year5,year6,practitioner}','Robbins & Cotran Pathologic Basis of Disease, 9th Ed, Ch 18: Viral Hepatitis, p.836'),
('{"medicine"}','Medicine','Gastroenterology','Pathology','Liver Disease - Hepatitis','easy','mcq','Ground-glass hepatocytes on haematoxylin and eosin stain are characteristic of which viral hepatitis?','["A. Hepatitis B (chronic infection)","B. Hepatitis A","C. Hepatitis C","D. Hepatitis E"]','A','Ground-glass hepatocytes are a characteristic histological finding in chronic hepatitis B, representing hepatocytes packed with HBsAg-containing smooth endoplasmic reticulum, giving a finely granular, ground-glass cytoplasmic appearance. Confirmed by orcein or Victoria blue staining.','universal',true,'{year1,year2,year3,year4,year5,year6,practitioner}','Robbins & Cotran Pathologic Basis of Disease, 9th Ed, Ch 18: Viral Hepatitis, p.836'),
('{"medicine"}','Medicine','Gastroenterology','Pathology','Liver Disease - Hepatitis','medium','mcq','IgM anti-HAV positivity in a patient with jaundice indicates:','["A. Past hepatitis A infection with lasting immunity","B. Acute hepatitis A infection","C. Hepatitis A vaccination","D. Cross-reaction from hepatitis B infection"]','B','IgM anti-HAV is the serological marker of acute hepatitis A infection, detectable from the onset of symptoms and persisting for 3-6 months. IgG anti-HAV indicates past infection or vaccination (immunity). There is no chronic carrier state for HAV.','universal',true,'{year1,year2,year3,year4,year5,year6,practitioner}','Robbins & Cotran Pathologic Basis of Disease, 9th Ed, Ch 18: Viral Hepatitis, p.836'),
('{"medicine"}','Medicine','Gastroenterology','Pathology','Liver Disease - Hepatitis','hard','mcq','Which pattern of hepatic histology indicates severe acute hepatitis progressing towards post-necrotic cirrhosis?','["A. Focal hepatocyte necrosis with lymphocytic portal inflammation","B. Zone 1 (periportal) necrosis only","C. Bridging (confluent) necrosis connecting portal tracts to terminal hepatic venules","D. Diffuse micro-vesicular steatosis"]','C','Bridging necrosis — confluent bands of hepatocyte necrosis connecting portal tracts to terminal hepatic venules (or portal-to-portal) — indicates severe hepatitis and is a precursor to post-necrotic scarring and cirrhosis. It portends a worse prognosis than focal lobular necrosis.','universal',true,'{year1,year2,year3,year4,year5,year6,practitioner}','Robbins & Cotran Pathologic Basis of Disease, 9th Ed, Ch 18: Viral Hepatitis, p.836'),
('{"medicine"}','Medicine','Gastroenterology','Pathology','Liver Disease - Hepatitis','medium','mcq','Which hepatitis virus is a defective RNA virus requiring co-infection with hepatitis B to replicate?','["A. Hepatitis C virus","B. Hepatitis G virus","C. Hepatitis E virus","D. Hepatitis D virus (delta agent)"]','D','Hepatitis D virus (HDV) is a defective single-stranded RNA virus requiring HBsAg from HBV for its envelope. Co-infection with HBV + HDV can cause more severe acute hepatitis; HDV superinfection of HBV chronic carriers markedly accelerates progression to cirrhosis.','universal',true,'{year1,year2,year3,year4,year5,year6,practitioner}','Robbins & Cotran Pathologic Basis of Disease, 9th Ed, Ch 18: Viral Hepatitis, p.836'),
('{"medicine"}','Medicine','Gastroenterology','Pathology','Liver Disease - Hepatitis','easy','mcq','Which serological marker confirms immunity to hepatitis B from vaccination or resolved natural infection?','["A. Anti-HBs","B. IgM anti-HBc","C. HBsAg","D. HBeAg"]','A','Anti-HBs (antibody to HBsAg) is the marker of immunity to HBV. It appears after resolution of acute infection or after vaccination. A level >10 mIU/mL indicates adequate seroprotection. Anti-HBs is the only marker present after vaccination (without anti-HBc).','universal',true,'{year1,year2,year3,year4,year5,year6,practitioner}','Robbins & Cotran Pathologic Basis of Disease, 9th Ed, Ch 18: Viral Hepatitis, p.836'),
('{"medicine"}','Medicine','Gastroenterology','Pathology','Liver Disease - Hepatitis','hard','mcq','Which hepatitis virus most commonly causes acute liver failure in pregnant women, particularly in the third trimester?','["A. Hepatitis A","B. Hepatitis E","C. Hepatitis C","D. Hepatitis B"]','B','Hepatitis E virus (HEV), transmitted faeco-orally, carries a 15-25% mortality rate in pregnant women in the third trimester due to fulminant hepatic failure. It is endemic in developing countries and caused by HEV genotypes 1 and 2.','universal',true,'{year1,year2,year3,year4,year5,year6,practitioner}','Robbins & Cotran Pathologic Basis of Disease, 9th Ed, Ch 18: Viral Hepatitis, p.836'),
('{"medicine"}','Medicine','Gastroenterology','Pathology','Liver Disease - Cirrhosis and Complications','easy','mcq','Portal hypertension in cirrhosis leads to which triad of clinical complications?','["A. Jaundice, encephalopathy, and coagulopathy","B. Cholestasis, pruritus, and xanthelasma","C. Ascites, oesophageal varices, and splenomegaly","D. Hepatorenal syndrome, HCC, and SBP only"]','C','Portal hypertension caused by cirrhotic distortion of hepatic architecture produces the classic triad of ascites (portal hypertension + hypoalbuminaemia), oesophageal/gastric varices (portosystemic shunting), and congestive splenomegaly.','universal',true,'{year1,year2,year3,year4,year5,year6,practitioner}','Robbins & Cotran Pathologic Basis of Disease, 9th Ed, Ch 18: Cirrhosis, p.836'),
('{"medicine"}','Medicine','Gastroenterology','Pathology','Liver Disease - Cirrhosis and Complications','medium','mcq','Spontaneous bacterial peritonitis (SBP) in cirrhotic ascites is diagnosed by which finding?','["A. Total protein >3 g/dL in ascitic fluid","B. pH <7.0 in ascitic fluid","C. Glucose <40 mg/dL in ascitic fluid","D. Polymorphonuclear (neutrophil) count >250 cells/mm³ in ascitic fluid"]','D','SBP is diagnosed when the PMN count in ascitic fluid exceeds 250 cells/mm³, regardless of culture result. The most common causative organisms are Gram-negative enteric bacteria (E. coli, Klebsiella). Empirical third-generation cephalosporin treatment should be started on clinical suspicion.','universal',true,'{year1,year2,year3,year4,year5,year6,practitioner}','Robbins & Cotran Pathologic Basis of Disease, 9th Ed, Ch 18: Cirrhosis, p.836'),
('{"medicine"}','Medicine','Gastroenterology','Pathology','Liver Disease - Cirrhosis and Complications','medium','mcq','Hepatorenal syndrome (HRS) in cirrhosis is best described as:','["A. Functional renal failure from renal vasoconstriction without intrinsic renal pathology","B. Acute tubular necrosis from hepatotoxic drugs","C. Membranous glomerulonephritis from immune complex deposition","D. Obstructive uropathy from hepatic encephalopathy"]','A','HRS is a form of functional renal failure in cirrhosis characterised by intense renal vasoconstriction (due to splanchnic vasodilation activating RAAS and SNS) without intrinsic renal pathology. Kidneys are structurally normal and recover function if transplanted. It carries very high short-term mortality.','universal',true,'{year1,year2,year3,year4,year5,year6,practitioner}','Robbins & Cotran Pathologic Basis of Disease, 9th Ed, Ch 18: Cirrhosis, p.836'),
('{"medicine"}','Medicine','Gastroenterology','Pathology','Liver Disease - Cirrhosis and Complications','hard','mcq','Which serum marker is most useful in screening for hepatocellular carcinoma (HCC) in cirrhotic patients?','["A. CEA","B. AFP (alpha-fetoprotein)","C. CA 19-9","D. CA 125"]','B','AFP is elevated in ~70% of HCC cases and is used alongside 6-monthly hepatic ultrasound for HCC surveillance in cirrhotic patients. AFP >400 ng/mL combined with characteristic imaging (arterial enhancement + washout) is diagnostic of HCC without biopsy.','universal',true,'{year1,year2,year3,year4,year5,year6,practitioner}','Robbins & Cotran Pathologic Basis of Disease, 9th Ed, Ch 18: Hepatocellular Carcinoma, p.836'),
('{"medicine"}','Medicine','Gastroenterology','Pathology','Liver Disease - Cirrhosis and Complications','easy','mcq','Which of the following is NOT included in the Child-Pugh scoring system for cirrhosis severity?','["A. Serum bilirubin","B. Serum albumin","C. Serum AFP","D. Prothrombin time (INR)"]','C','The Child-Pugh score includes: serum bilirubin, serum albumin, prothrombin time/INR, degree of ascites, and degree of hepatic encephalopathy. AFP is not a component. Child-Pugh class A/B/C predicts 1- and 2-year survival in cirrhosis.','universal',true,'{year1,year2,year3,year4,year5,year6,practitioner}','Robbins & Cotran Pathologic Basis of Disease, 9th Ed, Ch 18: Cirrhosis, p.836'),
('{"medicine"}','Medicine','Gastroenterology','Pathology','Liver Disease - Cirrhosis and Complications','medium','mcq','Oesophageal varices in portal hypertension arise because portal blood is diverted through which portosystemic anastomosis?','["A. Haemorrhoidal veins","B. Retroperitoneal collaterals","C. Paraumbilical veins (caput medusae)","D. Left gastric (coronary) vein → oesophageal submucosal venous plexus"]','D','Portal hypertension forces blood through the left gastric (coronary) vein into the oesophageal submucosal venous plexus, forming oesophageal varices. These thin-walled dilated veins risk rupture, causing life-threatening upper GI haemorrhage with mortality of 20-30% per episode.','universal',true,'{year1,year2,year3,year4,year5,year6,practitioner}','Robbins & Cotran Pathologic Basis of Disease, 9th Ed, Ch 18: Cirrhosis, p.836'),
('{"medicine"}','Medicine','Gastroenterology','Pathology','Liver Disease - Cirrhosis and Complications','hard','mcq','Which chronic liver disease is the most common aetiology of HCC in Sub-Saharan Africa and parts of Asia?','["A. Chronic HBV infection (with or without cirrhosis)","B. Alcoholic cirrhosis","C. Non-alcoholic fatty liver disease","D. Wilsons disease"]','A','Chronic HBV infection is the dominant HCC risk factor in Sub-Saharan Africa and Asia where HBV is endemic and perinatal transmission is common. HBV can cause HCC even without established cirrhosis, through viral DNA integration and oncogene activation. Aflatoxin B1 co-exposure further increases risk.','universal',true,'{year1,year2,year3,year4,year5,year6,practitioner}','Robbins & Cotran Pathologic Basis of Disease, 9th Ed, Ch 18: Hepatocellular Carcinoma, p.836'),
('{"medicine"}','Medicine','Gastroenterology','Pathology','Liver Disease - Cirrhosis and Complications','medium','mcq','Hepatic encephalopathy in cirrhosis is primarily mediated by which substance?','["A. Bilirubin","B. Ammonia derived from gut bacterial metabolism of proteins","C. Urobilinogen","D. Bile acids accumulating in the brain"]','B','Hepatic encephalopathy results primarily from elevated circulating ammonia (and other toxins) that bypass hepatic detoxification via portosystemic shunting and cross the blood-brain barrier, causing astrocyte swelling and cerebral dysfunction.','universal',true,'{year1,year2,year3,year4,year5,year6,practitioner}','Robbins & Cotran Pathologic Basis of Disease, 9th Ed, Ch 18: Cirrhosis, p.836'),
('{"medicine"}','Medicine','Gastroenterology','Pathology','Liver Disease - Cirrhosis and Complications','easy','mcq','Which finding on liver biopsy is characteristic of alcoholic hepatitis?','["A. Ground-glass hepatocytes","B. PAS-positive macrophages","C. Mallory-Denk bodies (eosinophilic cytoplasmic inclusions of ubiquitinated keratin)","D. Canalicular cholestasis with bile plugs"]','C','Mallory-Denk bodies are eosinophilic, irregular intracytoplasmic inclusions composed of ubiquitinated intermediate filaments (keratins 8 and 18). They are characteristic of alcoholic hepatitis and also seen in NASH, PBC, and Wilson''s disease.','universal',true,'{year1,year2,year3,year4,year5,year6,practitioner}','Robbins & Cotran Pathologic Basis of Disease, 9th Ed, Ch 18: Alcoholic Liver Disease, p.836'),
('{"medicine"}','Medicine','Gastroenterology','Pathology','Liver Disease - Cirrhosis and Complications','hard','mcq','The Milan criteria define eligibility for liver transplantation in which condition?','["A. Alcoholic cirrhosis with Child-Pugh C score","B. Primary biliary cholangitis with end-stage disease","C. Fulminant hepatic failure from paracetamol overdose","D. Hepatocellular carcinoma in cirrhosis (single nodule ≤5 cm or up to 3 nodules each ≤3 cm, no vascular invasion)"]','D','The Milan criteria define acceptable HCC burden for liver transplantation: single tumour ≤5 cm, or up to 3 tumours each ≤3 cm, with no macrovascular invasion or extrahepatic spread. Patients within Milan criteria have post-transplant 5-year survival comparable to transplantation for non-malignant disease.','universal',true,'{year1,year2,year3,year4,year5,year6,practitioner}','Robbins & Cotran Pathologic Basis of Disease, 9th Ed, Ch 18: Hepatocellular Carcinoma, p.836'),
('{"medicine"}','Medicine','Gastroenterology','Pathology','Biliary Disease','easy','mcq','What is the most common type of gallstone in Western populations?','["A. Cholesterol stones","B. Brown pigment stones","C. Black pigment stones","D. Mixed calcium bilirubinate stones"]','A','Cholesterol stones account for ~80% of gallstones in Western populations. They form when bile is supersaturated with cholesterol. Risk factors include: female sex, obesity, age, multiparity, OCP, and rapid weight loss (4Fs: Fat, Female, Forty, Fertile).','universal',true,'{year1,year2,year3,year4,year5,year6,practitioner}','Robbins & Cotran Pathologic Basis of Disease, 9th Ed, Ch 18: Cholelithiasis, p.836'),
('{"medicine"}','Medicine','Gastroenterology','Pathology','Biliary Disease','medium','mcq','Black pigment gallstones are associated with which underlying condition?','["A. Bacterial biliary infection","B. Chronic haemolytic anaemia (e.g., sickle cell disease, hereditary spherocytosis)","C. Obesity and dyslipidaemia","D. Oral contraceptive pill use"]','B','Black pigment stones form from unconjugated bilirubin (from excessive haemolysis) precipitating with calcium in sterile bile. They are associated with chronic haemolytic anaemias. Brown pigment stones form in infected bile from bacterial deconjugation of bilirubin.','universal',true,'{year1,year2,year3,year4,year5,year6,practitioner}','Robbins & Cotran Pathologic Basis of Disease, 9th Ed, Ch 18: Cholelithiasis, p.836'),
('{"medicine"}','Medicine','Gastroenterology','Pathology','Biliary Disease','medium','mcq','Murphy''s sign, elicited on physical examination, is a feature of which condition?','["A. Hepatocellular carcinoma","B. Cholangiocarcinoma","C. Acute cholecystitis","D. Primary sclerosing cholangitis"]','C','Murphy''s sign is inspiratory arrest during deep palpation of the right upper quadrant as the inflamed gallbladder descends against the examiner''s hand. It is a classic sign of acute cholecystitis, caused by gallstone impaction in the cystic duct with subsequent gallbladder wall inflammation.','universal',true,'{year1,year2,year3,year4,year5,year6,practitioner}','Robbins & Cotran Pathologic Basis of Disease, 9th Ed, Ch 18: Cholecystitis, p.836'),
('{"medicine"}','Medicine','Gastroenterology','Pathology','Biliary Disease','hard','mcq','Primary sclerosing cholangitis (PSC) is characterised by which histological finding and radiological appearance?','["A. Granulomatous inflammation; absent intrahepatic ducts on MRCP","B. Florid duct lesion with granulomata; pruned tree appearance on MRCP","C. Lymphocytic cholangitis without fibrosis; normal MRCP","D. Periductal onion-skin fibrosis causing segmental strictures; beaded appearance on MRCP"]','D','PSC is characterised by periductal onion-skin concentric fibrosis causing segmental stricturing and dilatation of both intra- and extrahepatic bile ducts, producing the classic beaded appearance on MRCP. It is associated with UC and carries significant risk of cholangiocarcinoma.','universal',true,'{year1,year2,year3,year4,year5,year6,practitioner}','Robbins & Cotran Pathologic Basis of Disease, 9th Ed, Ch 18: Primary Sclerosing Cholangitis, p.836'),
('{"medicine"}','Medicine','Gastroenterology','Pathology','Biliary Disease','easy','mcq','Primary biliary cholangitis (PBC) is associated with which autoantibody?','["A. Antimitochondrial antibody (AMA)","B. Anti-smooth muscle antibody (ASMA)","C. Anti-liver-kidney microsomal antibody (anti-LKM1)","D. Antinuclear antibody (ANA) only"]','A','Antimitochondrial antibody (AMA), directed against the E2 component of pyruvate dehydrogenase complex, is present in >95% of PBC patients and is highly specific. PBC predominantly affects middle-aged women presenting with fatigue and pruritus.','universal',true,'{year1,year2,year3,year4,year5,year6,practitioner}','Robbins & Cotran Pathologic Basis of Disease, 9th Ed, Ch 18: Primary Biliary Cholangitis, p.836'),
('{"medicine"}','Medicine','Gastroenterology','Pathology','Biliary Disease','medium','mcq','Courvoisier''s law states that a palpable non-tender gallbladder with painless obstructive jaundice suggests:','["A. Acute cholecystitis from gallstones","B. Malignant obstruction of the common bile duct (e.g., pancreatic head carcinoma)","C. Primary sclerosing cholangitis","D. Choledocholithiasis from Mirizzi syndrome"]','B','Courvoisier''s law: in obstructive jaundice, a palpable non-tender gallbladder suggests malignant (not gallstone) CBD obstruction, as chronic cholelithiasis produces a thick-walled fibrotic non-distensible gallbladder. A smooth distensible gallbladder indicates obstruction at the pancreatic head or ampulla.','universal',true,'{year1,year2,year3,year4,year5,year6,practitioner}','Robbins & Cotran Pathologic Basis of Disease, 9th Ed, Ch 18: Pancreatic/Biliary Carcinoma, p.836'),
('{"medicine"}','Medicine','Gastroenterology','Pathology','Biliary Disease','hard','mcq','Cholangiocarcinoma is most strongly associated with which risk factors?','["A. Gallstones and obesity","B. Hepatitis B infection and aflatoxin exposure","C. Primary sclerosing cholangitis, liver fluke infection (Opisthorchis/Clonorchis), and choledochal cysts","D. Alcohol use and non-alcoholic fatty liver disease"]','C','Cholangiocarcinoma risk factors include PSC (in Western countries), liver flukes (Opisthorchis viverrini, Clonorchis sinensis — in Southeast Asia), choledochal cysts, hepatolithiasis, and Caroli disease. CA 19-9 is elevated and MRCP/ERCP is used for diagnosis.','universal',true,'{year1,year2,year3,year4,year5,year6,practitioner}','Robbins & Cotran Pathologic Basis of Disease, 9th Ed, Ch 18: Cholangiocarcinoma, p.836'),
('{"medicine"}','Medicine','Gastroenterology','Pathology','Biliary Disease','medium','mcq','A 45-year-old woman with fatigue, pruritus, and cholestatic liver profile has positive AMA. Liver biopsy shows lymphocytic infiltration and destruction of small intrahepatic bile ducts (florid duct lesion). What is the diagnosis?','["A. Primary sclerosing cholangitis","B. Drug-induced cholestasis","C. Autoimmune hepatitis","D. Primary biliary cholangitis (PBC)"]','D','PBC is characterised by progressive destruction of small intrahepatic bile ducts by granulomatous (florid duct) lesions, positive AMA (>95%), elevated alkaline phosphatase, and cholestatic symptoms. It predominantly affects middle-aged women and progresses to cirrhosis over decades.','universal',true,'{year1,year2,year3,year4,year5,year6,practitioner}','Robbins & Cotran Pathologic Basis of Disease, 9th Ed, Ch 18: Primary Biliary Cholangitis, p.836'),
('{"medicine"}','Medicine','Gastroenterology','Pathology','Biliary Disease','easy','mcq','Acute acalculous cholecystitis most commonly occurs in which clinical setting?','["A. Critically ill patients (ICU, post-major surgery, trauma, burns, TPN)","B. Young women with obesity","C. Patients with sickle cell disease","D. Pregnancy in the third trimester"]','A','Acute acalculous cholecystitis occurs in the absence of gallstones in critically ill or debilitated patients, resulting from bile stasis, gallbladder ischaemia, and infection. It carries higher morbidity and mortality than calculous cholecystitis.','universal',true,'{year1,year2,year3,year4,year5,year6,practitioner}','Robbins & Cotran Pathologic Basis of Disease, 9th Ed, Ch 18: Cholecystitis, p.836'),
('{"medicine"}','Medicine','Gastroenterology','Pathology','Biliary Disease','hard','mcq','Mirizzi syndrome refers to which complication of cholelithiasis?','["A. Gallstone impaction in the terminal ileum causing small bowel obstruction","B. External compression of the common hepatic duct by a gallstone impacted in the cystic duct or Hartmanns pouch","C. Gallbladder perforation with peritonitis","D. Erosion of a gallstone into the duodenum causing a cholecystoduodenal fistula"]','B','Mirizzi syndrome occurs when a large gallstone impacted in the cystic duct or Hartmann''s pouch compresses the adjacent common hepatic duct, causing obstructive jaundice. It may mimic cholangiocarcinoma. Surgical management is complex due to distorted biliary anatomy.','universal',false,'{year1,year2,year3,year4,year5,year6,practitioner}','Robbins & Cotran Pathologic Basis of Disease, 9th Ed, Ch 18: Cholelithiasis, p.836'),
('{"medicine"}','Medicine','Gastroenterology','Pathology','Pancreatic Disease','easy','mcq','What are the two most common causes of acute pancreatitis?','["A. Hypertriglyceridaemia and hypercalcaemia","B. ERCP and autoimmune disease","C. Gallstones and alcohol","D. Trauma and drugs"]','C','Gallstones (45%) and alcohol (35%) together account for ~80% of acute pancreatitis cases. Gallstones cause pancreatitis by transient or persistent obstruction of the common bile duct/ampulla. Alcohol causes pancreatitis through toxic effects on acinar cells and duct obstruction.','universal',true,'{year1,year2,year3,year4,year5,year6,practitioner}','Robbins & Cotran Pathologic Basis of Disease, 9th Ed, Ch 17/18: Pancreatitis, p.836'),
('{"medicine"}','Medicine','Gastroenterology','Pathology','Pancreatic Disease','medium','mcq','Cullen''s sign and Grey Turner''s sign in severe acute pancreatitis indicate:','["A. Biliary obstruction and cholestasis","B. Ascending cholangitis complicating gallstone pancreatitis","C. Pancreatic pseudocyst formation","D. Retroperitoneal haemorrhage tracking to the periumbilical area (Cullens) and flanks (Grey Turners)"]','D','Cullen''s sign (periumbilical bruising) and Grey Turner''s sign (flank bruising) indicate retroperitoneal haemorrhage tracking along tissue planes in severe necrotising pancreatitis. They are associated with haemorrhagic pancreatitis and a poor prognosis, typically appearing 24-48 hours after onset.','universal',true,'{year1,year2,year3,year4,year5,year6,practitioner}','Robbins & Cotran Pathologic Basis of Disease, 9th Ed, Ch 17/18: Acute Pancreatitis, p.836'),
('{"medicine"}','Medicine','Gastroenterology','Pathology','Pancreatic Disease','medium','mcq','Which statement about Ranson''s criteria for acute pancreatitis is correct?','["A. A score ≥3 indicates severe pancreatitis with increased morbidity and mortality","B. A score ≥1 at admission indicates severe pancreatitis","C. All criteria are assessed at 48 hours only","D. The score includes serum bilirubin and prothrombin time"]','A','Ranson''s criteria: ≥3 indicates severe pancreatitis with significantly increased morbidity and mortality (~15% at ≥3, >50% at ≥7). Admission criteria include age >55, WBC >16,000/μL, glucose >200 mg/dL, LDH >350 IU/L, AST >250 IU/L. 48-hour criteria include haematocrit drop >10%, BUN rise, Ca²⁺ <8 mg/dL, PaO2 <60 mmHg.','universal',true,'{year1,year2,year3,year4,year5,year6,practitioner}','Robbins & Cotran Pathologic Basis of Disease, 9th Ed, Ch 17/18: Acute Pancreatitis, p.836'),
('{"medicine"}','Medicine','Gastroenterology','Pathology','Pancreatic Disease','hard','mcq','Which type of pancreatic carcinoma accounts for the majority of cases and characteristically presents with painless obstructive jaundice?','["A. Acinar cell carcinoma of the body","B. Ductal adenocarcinoma of the head of the pancreas","C. Mucinous cystic neoplasm of the tail","D. Intraductal papillary mucinous neoplasm of the main duct"]','B','Pancreatic ductal adenocarcinoma accounts for >85% of pancreatic cancers. Approximately 60-70% arise in the pancreatic head, where they obstruct the common bile duct causing painless progressive obstructive jaundice (dark urine, pale stools, pruritus). A distensible gallbladder (Courvoisier''s sign) may be palpable.','universal',true,'{year1,year2,year3,year4,year5,year6,practitioner}','Robbins & Cotran Pathologic Basis of Disease, 9th Ed, Ch 17/18: Pancreatic Carcinoma, p.836'),
('{"medicine"}','Medicine','Gastroenterology','Pathology','Pancreatic Disease','easy','mcq','Which tumour marker is most commonly elevated in pancreatic ductal adenocarcinoma?','["A. AFP","B. CEA","C. CA 19-9","D. CA 125"]','C','CA 19-9 is elevated in ~80% of patients with pancreatic ductal adenocarcinoma and is the most useful serum marker for monitoring treatment response and recurrence. However, it is not sufficiently sensitive/specific for screening and can be elevated in biliary obstruction.','universal',true,'{year1,year2,year3,year4,year5,year6,practitioner}','Robbins & Cotran Pathologic Basis of Disease, 9th Ed, Ch 17/18: Pancreatic Carcinoma, p.836'),
('{"medicine"}','Medicine','Gastroenterology','Pathology','Pancreatic Disease','medium','mcq','Chronic pancreatitis characteristically shows which finding on plain abdominal X-ray?','["A. Sentinel loop sign","B. Air in the biliary tree (aerobilia)","C. Paralytic ileus pattern","D. Calcification of the pancreas"]','D','Pancreatic calcification on plain abdominal X-ray is characteristic of chronic pancreatitis, resulting from calcium carbonate precipitation within protein plugs in pancreatic ducts. It is most commonly seen in alcoholic chronic pancreatitis.','universal',true,'{year1,year2,year3,year4,year5,year6,practitioner}','Robbins & Cotran Pathologic Basis of Disease, 9th Ed, Ch 17/18: Chronic Pancreatitis, p.836'),
('{"medicine"}','Medicine','Gastroenterology','Pathology','Pancreatic Disease','hard','mcq','Which hereditary mutation causes autosomal dominant hereditary pancreatitis with early-onset recurrent acute pancreatitis and high risk of pancreatic carcinoma?','["A. PRSS1 (cationic trypsinogen) gain-of-function mutation","B. CFTR mutation","C. SPINK1 loss-of-function mutation","D. CTRC (chymotrypsin C) mutation"]','A','Hereditary pancreatitis is most commonly caused by gain-of-function mutations in PRSS1 (cationic trypsinogen), which prevent auto-inactivation of trypsin, leading to premature intrapancreatic trypsin activation. Carriers have 40-70% lifetime risk of pancreatic ductal adenocarcinoma.','universal',true,'{year1,year2,year3,year4,year5,year6,practitioner}','Robbins & Cotran Pathologic Basis of Disease, 9th Ed, Ch 17/18: Pancreatitis, p.836'),
('{"medicine"}','Medicine','Gastroenterology','Pathology','Pancreatic Disease','medium','mcq','A pancreatic pseudocyst following acute pancreatitis differs from a true cyst in that it:','["A. Contains pancreatic enzymes and has a true epithelial lining","B. Lacks an epithelial lining and is walled off by fibrous and granulation tissue","C. Is always located in the head of the pancreas","D. Is always a premalignant lesion requiring resection"]','B','A pancreatic pseudocyst develops when pancreatic ductal disruption and enzyme leak become walled off by fibrous/granulation tissue (not epithelium). It contains amylase-rich fluid and typically develops 4-6 weeks after acute pancreatitis. Most resolve spontaneously; large/symptomatic ones require drainage.','universal',true,'{year1,year2,year3,year4,year5,year6,practitioner}','Robbins & Cotran Pathologic Basis of Disease, 9th Ed, Ch 17/18: Acute Pancreatitis, p.836'),
('{"medicine"}','Medicine','Gastroenterology','Pathology','Pancreatic Disease','easy','mcq','Chronic pancreatitis leads to exocrine insufficiency manifesting as:','["A. Diabetes mellitus from glucagon deficiency","B. Achlorhydria from gastrin deficiency","C. Steatorrhoea and fat-soluble vitamin deficiency from lipase deficiency","D. Bile salt malabsorption from cholecystokinin deficiency"]','C','Chronic pancreatitis destroys exocrine acini, causing lipase, amylase, and protease deficiency. Steatorrhoea (fat malabsorption) occurs when >90% of exocrine function is lost. Fat-soluble vitamin (A, D, E, K) deficiency results. Endocrine insufficiency (pancreatogenic diabetes) also occurs from islet destruction.','universal',true,'{year1,year2,year3,year4,year5,year6,practitioner}','Robbins & Cotran Pathologic Basis of Disease, 9th Ed, Ch 17/18: Chronic Pancreatitis, p.836'),
('{"medicine"}','Medicine','Gastroenterology','Pathology','Pancreatic Disease','hard','mcq','Main duct IPMN is considered high-risk for malignancy because:','["A. It always presents with abdominal pain and jaundice","B. Branch duct IPMN has higher malignant risk than main duct IPMN","C. It is caused by KRAS mutation alone without further progression","D. Main duct IPMN has ~70% risk of harbouring high-grade dysplasia or invasive carcinoma"]','D','Main duct IPMN involves the main pancreatic duct (dilated >5 mm) and carries approximately 70% risk of harbouring high-grade dysplasia or invasive carcinoma, warranting surgical resection. Branch duct IPMN has lower risk (~25%) and may be observed if <3 cm without worrisome features.','universal',true,'{year1,year2,year3,year4,year5,year6,practitioner}','Robbins & Cotran Pathologic Basis of Disease, 9th Ed, Ch 17/18: Pancreatic Carcinoma/IPMN, p.836'),
('{"medicine"}','Medicine','Gastroenterology','Pathology','Appendicitis and Peritonitis','easy','mcq','Rovsing''s sign in acute appendicitis is elicited by:','["A. Palpation of the left iliac fossa causing referred pain in the right iliac fossa","B. Deep palpation of the right iliac fossa causing local tenderness","C. Hyperextension of the right hip causing right iliac fossa pain","D. Internal rotation of the flexed right hip causing right iliac fossa pain"]','A','Rovsing''s sign: palpation of the left iliac fossa causes referred pain in the right iliac fossa, suggesting appendiceal peritoneal irritation. The psoas sign (right hip hyperextension causing pain) suggests a retrocaecal appendix. The obturator sign (internal rotation of flexed right hip) suggests a pelvic appendix.','universal',true,'{year1,year2,year3,year4,year5,year6,practitioner}','Robbins & Cotran Pathologic Basis of Disease, 9th Ed, Ch 17: Acute Appendicitis, p.816'),
('{"medicine"}','Medicine','Gastroenterology','Pathology','Appendicitis and Peritonitis','medium','mcq','The Alvarado score is used to assess the likelihood of acute appendicitis. Which of the following is NOT a component?','["A. Migration of pain to the right iliac fossa","B. Elevated serum amylase","C. Rebound tenderness in the right iliac fossa","D. Leukocytosis (WBC >10,000/μL)"]','B','The Alvarado (MANTRELS) score includes: Migration of pain to RIF (1), Anorexia (1), Nausea/vomiting (1), Tenderness in RIF (2), Rebound tenderness (1), Elevated temperature >37.3°C (1), Leukocytosis (2), Shift to left (1). Total=10. Serum amylase is NOT a component.','universal',true,'{year1,year2,year3,year4,year5,year6,practitioner}','Robbins & Cotran Pathologic Basis of Disease, 9th Ed, Ch 17: Acute Appendicitis, p.816'),
('{"medicine"}','Medicine','Gastroenterology','Pathology','Appendicitis and Peritonitis','medium','mcq','The most common causative organism in secondary bacterial peritonitis from appendiceal perforation is:','["A. Staphylococcus aureus","B. Streptococcus pneumoniae","C. Escherichia coli (Gram-negative enteric bacilli)","D. Clostridium difficile"]','C','Secondary peritonitis from perforated appendicitis is caused by faecal flora, predominantly Gram-negative enteric bacilli (E. coli most common) and anaerobes (Bacteroides fragilis). Polymicrobial infection is typical.','universal',true,'{year1,year2,year3,year4,year5,year6,practitioner}','Robbins & Cotran Pathologic Basis of Disease, 9th Ed, Ch 17: Peritoneal Infection, p.817'),
('{"medicine"}','Medicine','Gastroenterology','Pathology','Appendicitis and Peritonitis','hard','mcq','Which histological finding on appendix biopsy confirms the diagnosis of acute appendicitis?','["A. Lymphoid hyperplasia in the submucosa","B. Reactive mesothelial hyperplasia on the serosa","C. Eosinophilic infiltration of the mucosa only","D. Neutrophilic infiltration of the muscularis propria"]','D','The histological criterion for acute appendicitis is neutrophilic (PMN) infiltration of the muscularis propria. Mucosal neutrophilia alone is insufficient, as it can occur in other conditions. Transmural neutrophilic infiltration indicates gangrenous appendicitis.','universal',true,'{year1,year2,year3,year4,year5,year6,practitioner}','Robbins & Cotran Pathologic Basis of Disease, 9th Ed, Ch 17: Acute Appendicitis, p.816'),
('{"medicine"}','Medicine','Gastroenterology','Pathology','Appendicitis and Peritonitis','easy','mcq','What is the most common cause of luminal obstruction leading to acute appendicitis in adults?','["A. Faecolith (appendicolith)","B. Intestinal worm (Enterobius vermicularis)","C. Foreign body ingestion","D. Carcinoid tumour of the appendix"]','A','A faecolith (calcified faecal concretion/appendicolith) is the most common cause of appendiceal luminal obstruction in adults, leading to bacterial overgrowth, increased intraluminal pressure, vascular compromise, and acute appendicitis. In children, lymphoid hyperplasia is a more common cause.','universal',true,'{year1,year2,year3,year4,year5,year6,practitioner}','Robbins & Cotran Pathologic Basis of Disease, 9th Ed, Ch 17: Acute Appendicitis, p.816'),
('{"medicine"}','Medicine','Gastroenterology','Pathology','Appendicitis and Peritonitis','medium','mcq','Which complication arises when the appendix perforates and the omentum and adjacent bowel wall off the infection locally?','["A. Generalised peritonitis","B. Peri-appendiceal abscess","C. Pylephlebitis (septic portal vein thrombosis)","D. Psoas abscess"]','B','If perforation is contained by omentum and adjacent bowel, a peri-appendiceal abscess (localised collection of pus) forms. If containment fails, faecal peritonitis and generalised peritonitis result. Pylephlebitis (suppurative portal vein thrombosis) is a rare but life-threatening complication.','universal',true,'{year1,year2,year3,year4,year5,year6,practitioner}','Robbins & Cotran Pathologic Basis of Disease, 9th Ed, Ch 17: Acute Appendicitis, p.816'),
('{"medicine"}','Medicine','Gastroenterology','Pathology','Appendicitis and Peritonitis','hard','mcq','Pseudomyxoma peritonei most commonly arises from which primary tumour?','["A. Colorectal adenocarcinoma","B. Ovarian mucinous cystadenoma","C. Low-grade appendiceal mucinous neoplasm (LAMN)","D. Pancreatic mucinous cystic neoplasm"]','C','Pseudomyxoma peritonei — gelatinous mucinous material accumulating throughout the peritoneal cavity — most commonly originates from a ruptured low-grade appendiceal mucinous neoplasm (LAMN). Treatment is cytoreductive surgery with hyperthermic intraperitoneal chemotherapy (HIPEC).','universal',true,'{year1,year2,year3,year4,year5,year6,practitioner}','Robbins & Cotran Pathologic Basis of Disease, 9th Ed, Ch 17: Tumors of the Appendix, p.816'),
('{"medicine"}','Medicine','Gastroenterology','Pathology','Appendicitis and Peritonitis','medium','mcq','An Alvarado score of 7-10 in a patient with suspected appendicitis indicates:','["A. Appendicitis unlikely; discharge home","B. Equivocal findings — CT scan recommended","C. Score is not clinically validated","D. High probability of appendicitis — surgical consultation and likely appendicectomy"]','D','An Alvarado score of 7-10 indicates high probability of appendicitis and warrants surgical consultation and likely appendicectomy. A score of 5-6 is equivocal (CT/imaging recommended). A score ≤4 indicates low probability and observation/discharge may be appropriate.','universal',true,'{year1,year2,year3,year4,year5,year6,practitioner}','Robbins & Cotran Pathologic Basis of Disease, 9th Ed, Ch 17: Acute Appendicitis, p.816'),
('{"medicine"}','Medicine','Gastroenterology','Pathology','Appendicitis and Peritonitis','easy','mcq','The most common tumour of the appendix, which may obstruct the appendiceal lumen, is:','["A. Carcinoid tumour (well-differentiated neuroendocrine tumour)","B. Adenocarcinoma","C. Lipoma","D. Gastrointestinal stromal tumour"]','A','Carcinoid tumour (well-differentiated neuroendocrine tumour) is the most common appendiceal tumour, typically at the tip. Most are <2 cm and benign; metastasis is rare with tumours <2 cm. They may obstruct the lumen causing appendicitis or be found incidentally at appendicectomy.','universal',true,'{year1,year2,year3,year4,year5,year6,practitioner}','Robbins & Cotran Pathologic Basis of Disease, 9th Ed, Ch 17: Tumors of the Appendix, p.816'),
('{"medicine"}','Medicine','Gastroenterology','Pathology','Appendicitis and Peritonitis','hard','mcq','Primary peritonitis (without intra-abdominal source) in children is most commonly caused by:','["A. E. coli from gut translocation","B. Streptococcus pneumoniae","C. Bacteroides fragilis","D. Staphylococcus aureus from haematogenous spread"]','B','Primary peritonitis in children is most commonly caused by Streptococcus pneumoniae, with monomicrobial infection characteristic. It is thought to result from haematogenous or lymphatic seeding. In adults, SBP in cirrhosis is usually caused by Gram-negative enteric bacteria (E. coli).','universal',false,'{year1,year2,year3,year4,year5,year6,practitioner}','Robbins & Cotran Pathologic Basis of Disease, 9th Ed, Ch 17: Peritoneal Infection, p.817');
